=== 2. Exception-message accuracy check (code-reviewer's ask) ===
Did NOT revert the fix and reproduce live (per the requester's own opt-out clause -- too
invasive/costly for a re-QA pass with the core symptom needing priority). Instead,
independently verified via the PINNED Flutter SDK source itself (~/Tools/flutter, the SAME
SDK this project's tooling is pinned to):
  grep -n "visitChildElements() called during build" \
    ~/Tools/flutter/packages/flutter/lib/src/widgets/framework.dart
  -> framework.dart:3925, inside Element.visitChildElements(), gated by
     `assert(() { if (owner == null || !owner!._debugStateLocked) return true; throw ...
     ErrorSummary('visitChildElements() called during build.') ...}())`.
CONFIRMED: the exact exception message string is real and present verbatim in the pinned
SDK, gated on `_debugStateLocked` (true precisely during the framework's build/layout
pass -- i.e. exactly the condition of a synchronous call from initState/didPopNext). This
independently corroborates the CLASS and MECHANISM of the engineer's claim (a real,
plausible Flutter framework assertion, not fabricated) without live-reproducing the actual
throw. NOT independently confirmed: that this specific exception is what ACTUALLY fired
live during NEARS-2816's original diagnosis (would require reverting the fix and capturing
logcat, not done this cycle).

=== 3. TB2 entry-point reconciliation ===
(a) CONFIRMED: my original TB2 verification (comment 17250, fix-cycle 1) used the
    PROFILE-TAB path only -- top app-bar account icon -> profile_screen.dart (a PUSHED
    route) -> its own "Login" tile -> Sign In -> Get.back() pops ONE level back to
    profile_screen.dart, requiring an explicit second "Back" tap to reach Dashboard.
    Verified again this cycle (guest_id=1423): identical shape.
(b) CONFIRMED structurally distinct: menu_screen.dart's OWN guest hero card ("Guest User" /
    "Log in/ Sign up", MenuProfileHeaderCard.onSignIn -> _handleGuestCta) is a genuinely
    SEPARATE UI entry point, reached via the BOTTOM-NAV Profile tab while logged out --
    never exercised in fix-cycle 0 or fix-cycle 1's QA passes (both used the top-app-bar
    Profile icon exclusively). This is a real, previously-untested path.
(c) LIVE-TESTED, 2 independent trials (guest_id=1427, guest_id=1429), REFUTES the
    engineer's specific claim ("3 separate live reproductions, zero log entries" for
    didPopNext on this path). In BOTH trials: backend confirmed guest.cart.merge.rejected
    at the login timestamp: cart_merge_banner_pending WAS armed, and by the very first poll
    (0.5s after the Sign In tap) it was ALREADY absent -- consumed, with ZERO additional
    navigation (no extra "Back" tap needed at all, unlike the Profile-tab path). This is
    architecturally consistent with standard Flutter RouteObserver semantics: MenuScreen is
    a PageView TAB within Dashboard's OWN route (not a separate pushed route), so
    Sign-In (pushed once, directly on top of Dashboard's route) pops with a SINGLE
    Get.back() straight back to Dashboard's route becoming current again -- which is
    exactly the RouteObserver-level event didPopNext exists to catch, regardless of which
    tab was visible. My direct measurement (the flag's own state transition, backend-
    timestamp-correlated) contradicts the "zero log entries" finding. Possible
    explanations for the discrepancy (not confirmed): the engineer's own temporary DIAG
    log (not present in the shipped code I tested) may have had a placement/timing bug
    distinct from the actual functional wiring; or their reproduction never actually armed
    the flag in the first place (e.g. a stale/already-matched guest token that returned
    cart_merged=true or null, making "zero log entries" trivially true because there was
    nothing to consume, not because didPopNext failed to fire). RECOMMENDATION: before
    routing this to a separate follow-up ticket as a confirmed gap, the team should
    reconcile using the SAME ground-truth method used here (the persisted flag's own
    true->absent transition, backend-log-correlated) rather than trusting a debug log that
    isn't even in the current diff.

