=== Grounded hypothesis for the next fix cycle (source-code-reasoned, NOT live-instrumented) ===
Read lib/features/dashboard/screens/dashboard_screen.dart and
lib/features/home/screens/home_screen.dart directly. Confirmed via source:
  - DashboardScreen's OWN Scaffold (dashboard_screen.dart ~line 628) owns
    `bottomNavigationBar: NBottomNav(...)` (the REAL visible bottom-nav chrome) and
    `body: PageView.builder(...)` containing `_screens[index]` (one of which is HomeScreen).
  - HomeScreen ITSELF independently builds its OWN, separate, nested Scaffold
    (home_screen.dart ~line 238, `backgroundColor: Colors.transparent`) INSIDE that
    PageView page.
  - NSnackBar.show(Get.context!, ...) calls `ScaffoldMessenger.of(context)` -- a STANDARD
    Flutter UPWARD/ancestor InheritedWidget lookup from the given context. `Get.context`
    is the ROOT Navigator's own element (navigatorKey: Get.key) -- ABOVE/OUTSIDE both of
    the nested Scaffolds above (they are DESCENDANTS of Get.context, not ancestors).
    hasVisibleScaffold()'s own doc comment already states this exact fact for a DIFFERENT
    reason ("the ancestor lookup Scaffold.maybeOf(Get.context!) is null on every screen,
    healthy or not" -- which is why it does a DOWNWARD walk instead). The SAME structural
    fact very plausibly means ScaffoldMessenger.of(Get.context!) resolves to whatever
    (if anything) sits ABOVE the Navigator at the MaterialApp level -- not either of the
    two real, visible, per-screen Scaffolds -- which would explain a SnackBar being
    "shown" (called, no crash) yet never painting into either Scaffold's actual visible
    real estate. NOT live-confirmed (would need Flutter DevTools widget-inspector access
    to trace which ScaffoldMessengerState instance actually receives the showSnackBar call
    and whether any Scaffold is registered "current" with it) -- flagging as the most
    concrete, source-grounded lead for the next diagnosis pass, given 3 consecutive fix
    cycles targeting WHEN/WHERE the call fires have not changed the live symptom at all.
